# Open a capped bottle using both arms: one holds the bottle, the other
# brings the opener down onto the cap and pushes.
#
# bottle_id names a station in bartender_open's STATIONS table ('beer' is
# the only one that exists now, and the only one in the scene that has a
# cap). It is a string rather than an enum for the same reason
# PourDrink.bottle_id is: so adding a second capped bottle does not change
# the interface.
string bottle_id

# Put the bottle back in its stand and the opener back on its post when the
# cap is off. False leaves both arms holding what they picked up, which is
# what you want when the next thing to happen is a pour from that bottle.
bool stow_after
---
# Result
bool success
string message

# Where the cap ended up, measured from the bottle's mouth after the opener
# was lifted clear. This is the evidence that the bottle is actually open
# rather than the sequence merely having run: a cap that is still sitting on
# the lip reads near zero here, and success is false.
float32 cap_displacement_m

# How far the bottle moved while it was being pushed on. Small is the point
# -- it is the measure of how well the holding arm did its job -- and it is
# reported whether or not the open succeeded, because it is the first thing
# to look at when one fails.
float32 bottle_shift_m
---
# Feedback: coarse state machine progress, matching PourDrink's convention
# so a UI can narrate both actions the same way.
string state
float32 progress
